WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.970
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:56.865 --> 00:00:58.490
MARCUS EAST: Good
afternoon, everybody.

00:00:58.490 --> 00:01:00.850
My name's Marcus East.

00:01:00.850 --> 00:01:02.530
I have the best
job in the world,

00:01:02.530 --> 00:01:04.738
in that I'm the Chief
Technology Officer for National

00:01:04.738 --> 00:01:05.830
Geographic.

00:01:05.830 --> 00:01:07.600
And what you just
saw on screen there

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:12.230
was just a slice of the National
Geographic image collection.

00:01:12.230 --> 00:01:17.260
Now, we don't think of those
assets just as digital files.

00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:19.630
Every single one of those
pictures that you saw

00:01:19.630 --> 00:01:21.090
tells a story.

00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:23.080
They're the stories
of adventurers who've

00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:26.530
been around the world to
explore unknown places,

00:01:26.530 --> 00:01:28.840
photographers who
are trying to capture

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:31.150
their knowledge of the
world, or researchers

00:01:31.150 --> 00:01:33.520
who are trying to help
us better understand

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:35.090
the planet around us.

00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:38.560
And we take our responsibility
at National Geographic

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:42.220
very seriously as the
custodians of those images

00:01:42.220 --> 00:01:44.800
because those images
help to tell stories,

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:47.440
and those stories help to
improve people's knowledge

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:50.650
of the planet Earth, of
its people, its animals,

00:01:50.650 --> 00:01:52.130
and its places.

00:01:52.130 --> 00:01:54.760
And by doing so, we hope that
we can help people to care more

00:01:54.760 --> 00:01:57.520
about the planet because if
they care more about the planet,

00:01:57.520 --> 00:02:00.800
they're more likely
to protect the planet.

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:04.750
And so our team is incredibly
excited that not only do we

00:02:04.750 --> 00:02:07.715
do things that are fantastic
from a technology perspective,

00:02:07.715 --> 00:02:10.090
but we have an opportunity to
do things that are actually

00:02:10.090 --> 00:02:13.340
meaningful to society.

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:16.240
So those beautiful images, many
of which that you recognized,

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:20.615
obviously have to be supported
by a great technology platform.

00:02:20.615 --> 00:02:25.210
And at National
Geographic, we had what

00:02:25.210 --> 00:02:27.570
we called a system called SPI.

00:02:27.570 --> 00:02:31.630
And SPI was effectively the
home for these wonderful images.

00:02:31.630 --> 00:02:34.720
It had roughly 17
terabytes of data,

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:37.750
housing two million
iconic images

00:02:37.750 --> 00:02:40.000
that have really helped
over the last 130 years

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:45.010
to shape the narrative coming
out of National Geographic.

00:02:45.010 --> 00:02:46.690
It probably won't
surprise you to hear

00:02:46.690 --> 00:02:50.050
though that over the last
20 years, the team that's

00:02:50.050 --> 00:02:54.610
responsible for running our
image collection had built--

00:02:54.610 --> 00:02:57.325
they would describe it
as lovingly hand built--

00:02:57.325 --> 00:03:01.930
an application in Java that
was over a period of 20 years

00:03:01.930 --> 00:03:03.700
iterated upon.

00:03:03.700 --> 00:03:06.547
We discovered there was not much
documentation unfortunately.

00:03:06.547 --> 00:03:08.380
I'm sure that's a
situation that none of you

00:03:08.380 --> 00:03:11.710
have ever experienced before.

00:03:11.710 --> 00:03:13.510
It was hosted on premise.

00:03:13.510 --> 00:03:15.970
And it was managed
by an operations team

00:03:15.970 --> 00:03:18.580
without technical oversight.

00:03:18.580 --> 00:03:21.250
And from my perspective,
when I first

00:03:21.250 --> 00:03:24.520
joined National Geographic two
years ago, it was obvious to me

00:03:24.520 --> 00:03:28.030
that it had what we describe
as a suboptimal information

00:03:28.030 --> 00:03:29.020
security posture.

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:37.150
Now, that's a situation that was
clearly not acceptable to us.

00:03:37.150 --> 00:03:39.610
And interestingly,
that system that

00:03:39.610 --> 00:03:43.030
was housing the crown jewels
of National Geographic

00:03:43.030 --> 00:03:45.340
found itself under
intense scrutiny.

00:03:45.340 --> 00:03:47.560
And our VP of Information
Security came to me.

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:51.100
And she said, Marcus, I think
we have a bit of a problem.

00:03:51.100 --> 00:03:53.800
This system is not
really fit for purpose.

00:03:53.800 --> 00:03:56.530
In terms of the legacy
that we're protecting,

00:03:56.530 --> 00:03:58.850
we need to do
something about this.

00:03:58.850 --> 00:04:00.490
And we unfortunately
took the decision

00:04:00.490 --> 00:04:03.070
to take that system offline.

00:04:03.070 --> 00:04:04.960
And we worked with
the business team

00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:07.040
to come up with a solution.

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:09.435
We helped them to
remediate the issues.

00:04:09.435 --> 00:04:11.980
And after many,
many months of this,

00:04:11.980 --> 00:04:15.655
we realized that this was
actually not going to work.

00:04:15.655 --> 00:04:18.610
And we had to take
some drastic action.

00:04:18.610 --> 00:04:23.830
Our CEO came to my
office and said, Marcus,

00:04:23.830 --> 00:04:25.810
we need to do something here.

00:04:25.810 --> 00:04:28.450
I'd like your team to take
responsibility for this.

00:04:28.450 --> 00:04:31.720
And this was in
November of 2018.

00:04:31.720 --> 00:04:33.760
Your team has to take
responsibility for this,

00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:38.080
has to fix this application,
and get it back online.

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:40.720
And as he was leaving my office,
and he said, and by the way,

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:44.320
it must be up by January 2019.

00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:46.555
So that's obviously
not something

00:04:46.555 --> 00:04:47.680
that you ever want to hear.

00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:49.810
We had a situation
where the crown

00:04:49.810 --> 00:04:53.115
jewels of our organization were
in a system that needed help.

00:04:53.115 --> 00:04:56.080
We had a very limited time
in which to do something.

00:04:56.080 --> 00:04:59.440
And the thing that was driving
the need to do something

00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:02.380
was what we call our
Storyteller's Summit, where

00:05:02.380 --> 00:05:05.110
all of the famous photographers
that tell these National

00:05:05.110 --> 00:05:07.150
Geographic stories
around the world

00:05:07.150 --> 00:05:10.150
were coming to Washington
DC to National Geographic

00:05:10.150 --> 00:05:13.960
headquarters in order to
celebrate photography.

00:05:13.960 --> 00:05:15.460
And obviously,
that was not going

00:05:15.460 --> 00:05:18.340
to be a great story if we
didn't have the archive up

00:05:18.340 --> 00:05:19.570
and running.

00:05:19.570 --> 00:05:21.455
The good news is
we like a challenge

00:05:21.455 --> 00:05:23.080
in the Products and
Technology Function

00:05:23.080 --> 00:05:24.352
at National Geographic.

00:05:24.352 --> 00:05:25.810
In fact, two of my
colleagues here,

00:05:25.810 --> 00:05:28.270
Melissa Wiley, our VP
of Digital Products,

00:05:28.270 --> 00:05:31.390
and Mike Debnam, our
VP of engineering,

00:05:31.390 --> 00:05:32.650
huddled with me in my office.

00:05:32.650 --> 00:05:34.733
And we said, what are we
going to do here in order

00:05:34.733 --> 00:05:36.430
to resolve this problem?

00:05:36.430 --> 00:05:37.875
We didn't have much time.

00:05:37.875 --> 00:05:41.470
We were familiar with the cloud.

00:05:41.470 --> 00:05:44.500
We had spent a lot of time
working on a migration

00:05:44.500 --> 00:05:45.680
to the cloud.

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:47.680
But we realized that this
needed to be something

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:50.440
special, something more.

00:05:50.440 --> 00:05:55.012
We decided to reach out to
our friends at Google Cloud.

00:05:55.012 --> 00:05:56.470
And the reason that
we did that was

00:05:56.470 --> 00:06:00.070
not just because we felt that
they had incredible technology

00:06:00.070 --> 00:06:02.950
and a great solution to the
problems that we were facing,

00:06:02.950 --> 00:06:06.003
but we felt there was
an alignment of values.

00:06:06.003 --> 00:06:07.420
In our conversations
with them, we

00:06:07.420 --> 00:06:09.753
understood that they cared
as much about the environment

00:06:09.753 --> 00:06:10.750
as we do.

00:06:10.750 --> 00:06:13.210
Things like
sustainability were things

00:06:13.210 --> 00:06:16.630
that they talked about when
proposing to us using Google

00:06:16.630 --> 00:06:19.030
Cloud as a possible platform.

00:06:19.030 --> 00:06:23.530
And we then set about,
just before Thanksgiving,

00:06:23.530 --> 00:06:27.040
the process of putting this
incredibly valuable archive

00:06:27.040 --> 00:06:28.900
back online.

00:06:28.900 --> 00:06:31.647
Google worked very closely
with us, both from a technology

00:06:31.647 --> 00:06:33.730
perspective, but also from
a professional services

00:06:33.730 --> 00:06:34.970
perspective.

00:06:34.970 --> 00:06:37.780
We introduced a third
party called Globant.

00:06:37.780 --> 00:06:39.772
And, in fact, that was
Google's suggestion.

00:06:39.772 --> 00:06:41.980
We already had an existing
relationship with Globant,

00:06:41.980 --> 00:06:44.465
so we were very happy
to work with them.

00:06:44.465 --> 00:06:46.090
And you can imagine
what the next eight

00:06:46.090 --> 00:06:50.800
weeks was like with a team of
people working around the clock

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:54.910
to take this legacy application,
to move it into Google Cloud,

00:06:54.910 --> 00:06:56.320
to protect it.

00:06:56.320 --> 00:06:58.330
In this case, we
used technologies

00:06:58.330 --> 00:06:59.980
from both [? Oracle ?]
and Akamai

00:06:59.980 --> 00:07:03.520
to protect access
to that application.

00:07:03.520 --> 00:07:05.650
And we were able to get
it up and running in time

00:07:05.650 --> 00:07:07.600
for the Storyteller Summit.

00:07:07.600 --> 00:07:09.750
Now that's not something
that we would necessarily

00:07:09.750 --> 00:07:10.868
want to do again.

00:07:10.868 --> 00:07:13.410
I would not encourage anyone in
the room who's a technologist

00:07:13.410 --> 00:07:15.480
to sign up for an
eight week mission

00:07:15.480 --> 00:07:19.500
critical deployment to
the cloud, but we did it.

00:07:19.500 --> 00:07:21.810
And I would say that
it was incredibly

00:07:21.810 --> 00:07:24.300
rewarding to work
with both Google Cloud

00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:27.178
and Globant to get that done.

00:07:27.178 --> 00:07:28.930
You can't have a
CTO on the stage

00:07:28.930 --> 00:07:31.773
without an architectural
diagram, of course.

00:07:31.773 --> 00:07:33.190
I don't have time
to go into this.

00:07:33.190 --> 00:07:36.460
But what I would say is that
one of the beauties for us

00:07:36.460 --> 00:07:39.160
in working with Google
Cloud was the flexibility

00:07:39.160 --> 00:07:40.270
that they brought.

00:07:40.270 --> 00:07:42.160
I think when Tarik made
his opening comments,

00:07:42.160 --> 00:07:45.130
he really brought that
home with this focus

00:07:45.130 --> 00:07:46.840
on solving business problems.

00:07:46.840 --> 00:07:48.907
The fact that we had
other systems that

00:07:48.907 --> 00:07:50.740
were connected to SPI
and other systems that

00:07:50.740 --> 00:07:53.110
were dependent on it
wasn't an inhibitor

00:07:53.110 --> 00:07:56.990
to working with Google Cloud
to get this application up

00:07:56.990 --> 00:07:57.490
and running.

00:08:00.308 --> 00:08:01.350
So what about the future?

00:08:01.350 --> 00:08:04.530
We've obviously been able
to take this application

00:08:04.530 --> 00:08:06.990
and get it into
Google Cloud, make

00:08:06.990 --> 00:08:08.440
it available to
our photographers

00:08:08.440 --> 00:08:10.217
and our internal
stakeholders again.

00:08:10.217 --> 00:08:11.300
And that's been wonderful.

00:08:11.300 --> 00:08:15.210
In fact, I received hugs and
kisses from our photographers

00:08:15.210 --> 00:08:17.232
as a result of us
having done that.

00:08:17.232 --> 00:08:18.690
But there is an
interesting future.

00:08:18.690 --> 00:08:21.390
One of the reasons that we
chose to work with Google Cloud

00:08:21.390 --> 00:08:25.500
was our excitement about tools
such as AutoML and the Cloud

00:08:25.500 --> 00:08:28.410
Vision API that would allow us
to do things such as enriching

00:08:28.410 --> 00:08:31.110
the metadata that goes
with these wonderful images

00:08:31.110 --> 00:08:33.960
that we have in our
image collection.

00:08:33.960 --> 00:08:36.750
And we believe that we can
utilize the power of GCP

00:08:36.750 --> 00:08:39.390
and some of the native
capabilities in Google Cloud

00:08:39.390 --> 00:08:42.450
to build even more compelling
stories and even more

00:08:42.450 --> 00:08:45.340
compelling experiences
in the future.

00:08:45.340 --> 00:08:47.820
But the most important
thing is that the legacy

00:08:47.820 --> 00:08:50.910
of our photographers
is now safe.

00:08:50.910 --> 00:08:52.410
And these are not
just the legacies

00:08:52.410 --> 00:08:53.850
of these individual
photographers.

00:08:53.850 --> 00:08:58.090
In some respects, they are
part of the legacy of humanity.

00:08:58.090 --> 00:08:59.870
These are stories
that need to be told

00:08:59.870 --> 00:09:01.860
that people need to understand.

00:09:01.860 --> 00:09:04.440
And I'm delighted to say that
working with Google Cloud,

00:09:04.440 --> 00:09:06.930
we were able to secure
the image collection not

00:09:06.930 --> 00:09:10.290
just for ourselves, but for
many, many generations to come.

00:09:10.290 --> 00:09:13.340
[MUSIC PLAYING]

